### A · Development and its measures

1. The "development era" is often dated from US President **Truman**'s "**Point Four**" speech of ==20 January 1949==.
2. **Mahbub ul Haq** created the UNDP **Human Development Report** in ==1990==: development means "**enlarging people's choices**".
3. **Amartya Sen** wrote *Development as Freedom* (==1999==) — development as expanding **capabilities**.
4. **GDP** ignores **unpaid care and subsistence work**, most of it done by women.
5. **Marilyn Waring** showed in *If Women Counted* (==1988==) that national accounts make women's work invisible.
6. The **HDI** measures health, education and income — it is **not** a gender index.
7. UNDP introduced the **GDI** and **GEM** in ==1995== and the **Gender Inequality Index (GII)** in ==2010==.
8. The World Economic Forum's **Global Gender Gap Index** began in ==2006==.
9. Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026, and ==144th== on economic participation.
10. The World Bank's *World Development Report 2012* called gender equality "**smart economics**".
11. **Chant and Sweetman** (==2012==) criticised smart economics for treating women as **instruments of growth**.
12. In ==1968== **Mahbub ul Haq** said Pakistan's wealth was concentrated in about "**22 families**".

### B · Colonial perspectives

13. **James Mill** (*History of British India*, ==1817==) used women's status as the measure of **civilisation**.
14. **Katherine Mayo**'s *Mother India* (==1927==) was called by Gandhi "a **drain inspector's report**".
15. **Spivak** (==1988==): "White men are saving **brown women from brown men**."
16. **Lata Mani** showed women were the "**ground**", not the subjects, of the *sati* debate.
17. **Sati** was banned by the Bengal Sati Regulation of ==1829==.
18. The **Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act** was passed in ==1856==.
19. The **Indian Penal Code** (==1860==) remains the base of Pakistan's criminal law.
20. The **Criminal Tribes Act** (==1871==) labelled *hijras* a criminal class.
21. The **Punjab Laws Act** (==1872==) made **custom** the first rule, so many Muslim women lost Islamic inheritance.
22. The **Age of Consent Act** (==1891==) raised the age for girls from 10 to 12.
23. The **Child Marriage Restraint Act** ("Sarda Act") was passed in ==1929==.
24. Colonial **land settlements** recorded land in the names of **male heads** of households.
25. **Partha Chatterjee** (==1989==) described the nationalist division of life into an **outer material** and an **inner spiritual** world guarded by women.

### C · Capitalist perspectives and coloniality

26. **Engels** (==1884==) linked women's subordination to **private property**.
27. Industrial capitalism created the **public/private split** and the male **breadwinner** model.
28. **Maria Mies** (*Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale*, ==1986==) coined "**housewifisation**".
29. Mies studied the **lace makers of Narsapur**, India.
30. **Silvia Federici** (*Caliban and the Witch*, ==2004==) linked early capitalism to the **witch-hunts**.
31. **Aníbal Quijano** (==2000==) distinguished colonialism from **coloniality of power**.
32. **María Lugones** (==2007==) described the **coloniality of gender**.
33. **Arturo Escobar** wrote *Encountering Development* (==1995==), a post-development critique.
34. **Wolfgang Sachs** edited *The Development Dictionary* (==1992==).
35. **Chandra Mohanty**'s "**Under Western Eyes**" (==1984==) criticised the image of the "Third World woman" as victim.
36. The **Ali Enterprises** factory fire in **Karachi** (==11 September 2012==) killed about 260 workers.

### D · Modernisation theory

37. **W. W. Rostow** wrote *The Stages of Economic Growth: A Non-Communist Manifesto* (==1960==).
38. Rostow's five stages: **traditional society**, **preconditions**, **take-off**, **drive to maturity**, **high mass consumption**.
39. Modernisation theory blames **internal cultural factors** — tradition — for women's subordination (2021 question).
40. **Daniel Lerner** wrote *The Passing of Traditional Society* (==1958==).
41. **Inkeles and Smith** wrote *Becoming Modern* (==1974==).
42. Modernisation assumed benefits would "**trickle down**" to women through the family.
43. **Ester Boserup** (*Woman's Role in Economic Development*, ==1970==) showed modernisation often **marginalised** women farmers.
44. Boserup contrasted **female farming systems** (hoe) with **male farming systems** (plough).
45. In Pakistan the **Green Revolution** of the 1960s gave machines and credit mainly to **male landowners**.

### E · Dependency and world-system theory

46. **Raúl Prebisch** argued that the South's **terms of trade** decline (the Prebisch–Singer thesis).
47. **Andre Gunder Frank** coined "the **development of underdevelopment**" (==1966==).
48. **Walter Rodney** wrote *How Europe Underdeveloped Africa* (==1972==).
49. **Cardoso and Faletto** described "**dependent development**" (==1969==).
50. The UN called for a **New International Economic Order** in ==1974==.
51. The gender critique of dependency theory: it is **gender-blind** and ignores **local patriarchy**.
52. **World-System Theory** was developed by **Immanuel Wallerstein** (MCQ 2019) in *The Modern World-System* (==1974==).
53. Wallerstein divided the world into **core**, **semi-periphery** and **periphery**.
54. **Smith and Wallerstein** (==1992==) showed that households' **unpaid work** lets employers pay below a living wage.
55. **Fuentes and Ehrenreich** (==1983==) described the **global assembly line**.
56. **Kathryn Ward** (==1990==) brought women workers into world-system analysis.
57. **Gendered globalisation** means globalisation relies on and reshapes gender inequality (2025 question).
58. The **Rana Plaza** collapse in Bangladesh (==24 April 2013==) killed more than 1,100 garment workers.

### F · Structural functionalism

59. Structural functionalism is related to the **stability** of society (MCQ 2019).
60. **Émile Durkheim** and **Talcott Parsons** are the main functionalist thinkers.
61. **George Murdock** (==1949==) named four functions of the family: **sexual, reproductive, economic, educational**.
62. **Parsons and Bales** (==1955==): men play the **instrumental** role, women the **expressive** role.
63. **Robert Merton** added **manifest** and **latent** functions and **dysfunctions**.
64. The main gender critique: functionalism **justifies inequality** and ignores **power and conflict**.
65. **Ann Oakley** studied housework as real work in *The Sociology of Housework* (==1974==).

### G · WID

66. The concept that women have an important role in the development process is **WID** (MCQ 2019).
67. The term **WID** was coined by development professionals based in **Washington** (MCQ 2018).
68. The **Percy Amendment** (==1973==) required US aid to integrate women.
69. **USAID** created an Office of Women in Development in ==1974==.
70. WID drew on **liberal feminism** and **modernisation** theory.
71. The approach that takes the **sexual division of labour for granted** is **WID** (MCQ 2024).
72. WID was criticised mainly for **failing to transform gender relations** (MCQ 2026).
73. WID's projects were criticised as "**add women and stir**".
74. Pakistan's **Women's Division** was created in ==1979==; the **First Women Bank** opened in ==1989==.

### H · WAD

75. **WAD** (late 1970s) holds that women were always in development, but on **unequal, exploitative** terms.
76. WAD drew on **Marxism** and **dependency theory**.
77. The IDS workshop on "**The Continuing Subordination of Women**" was held in ==1978==.
78. WAD's main weakness: it stressed **class** and ignored **patriarchy** within classes.
79. The **2026** question on NGO-isation asked candidates to use the **WAD critique**.

### I · GAD, needs and empowerment

80. **GAD** (1980s) focuses on **gender relations** of power between women and men.
81. GAD focuses on "**all of these**" — gender relations, power and roles (MCQ 2025).
82. **DAWN** was founded in **Bangalore** in ==1984==; Sen and Grown presented its platform at Nairobi (==1985==).
83. **Maxine Molyneux** (==1985==) distinguished **practical** and **strategic** gender interests.
84. **Caroline Moser** turned them into **practical and strategic gender needs** (==1989==, ==1993==).
85. **Practical gender needs** address **immediate necessities** (MCQ 2026) — water, food, health.
86. **Strategic gender interests** aim to **change** women's position — land, laws, power.
87. Moser's **triple role**: **productive**, **reproductive** and **community-managing** work.
88. Moser's five policy approaches: **welfare, equity, anti-poverty, efficiency, empowerment**.
89. The author of *Reversed Realities* (==1994==) is **Naila Kabeer** (MCQ 2018).
90. Kabeer (==1999==): empowerment is the ability to make **strategic life choices** — **resources, agency, achievements**.
91. Transforming grassroots women from beneficiaries to **active participants** is **empowerment** (MCQ 2023).
92. **Sara Longwe**'s framework: **welfare, access, conscientisation, participation, control**.
93. The **Harvard Analytical Framework** (==1985==) maps activities and **access to and control over** resources.
94. The first global framework to adopt **gender mainstreaming** was the **Beijing** Declaration (==1995==) (MCQ 2024).
95. ECOSOC defined gender mainstreaming in ==1997==.
96. A benefit of gender mainstreaming: "**all of these**" (MCQ 2025).
97. **"All women are organised for an equitable share of development"** is an **incorrect** statement (MCQ 2023).

### J · Structural adjustment

98. The **debt crisis** broke out when **Mexico** could not pay in ==1982==.
99. **John Williamson** coined "**Washington Consensus**" in ==1989==.
100. SAP conditions: spending cuts, subsidy removal, **user fees**, devaluation, privatisation, liberalisation.
101. **Diane Elson** (*Male Bias in the Development Process*, ==1991==) said SAPs treat women's labour as "**infinitely elastic**".
102. **UNICEF**'s *Adjustment with a Human Face* was published in ==1987==.
103. The Commonwealth's *Engendering Adjustment for the 1990s* appeared in ==1989==.
104. **Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers** followed in ==1999==.
105. Pakistan's first IMF adjustment programme began in ==1988==; a **7-billion-dollar EFF** was approved in ==2024==.

### K · Globalisation

106. The growing **interdependence** of societies across the world is **globalisation** (MCQ 2023).
107. **Giddens**: globalisation is "the intensification of worldwide social relations which link distant localities".
108. The **WTO** was founded in ==1995==.
109. **Guy Standing** described the **feminisation of labour** (==1989==).
110. **Arlie Hochschild** described **global care chains** (==2000==).
111. **Naila Kabeer**'s *The Power to Choose* (==2000==) studied Bangladeshi garment workers.
112. The **Atlanta Agreement** (==1997==) moved Sialkot's football stitching into centres; many women homeworkers lost work.
113. The EU's **GSP+** for Pakistan (from ==2014==) requires 27 conventions including **CEDAW**.
114. **Sindh** passed Pakistan's first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==.
115. Pakistan's remittances reached a record of more than **38 billion dollars** in 2024–25.

### L · Climate and the global goals

116. **Françoise d'Eaubonne** coined **ecofeminism** in ==1974==.
117. **Vandana Shiva** wrote *Staying Alive* (==1988==).
118. **Bina Agarwal** (==1992==) proposed **feminist environmentalism**: women's link to nature is **material**.
119. The ==2022== floods affected about ==33 million== Pakistanis, including about ==650,000== pregnant women (UNFPA).
120. Pakistan emits **less than 1%** of global greenhouse gases.
121. The **loss and damage** fund was agreed at **COP27** (==2022==).
122. The **Lima Work Programme on Gender** was adopted in ==2014==; the UNFCCC **Gender Action Plan** in ==2017==.
123. The MDGs (==2000==) had **8** goals; **MDG 3** was gender equality.
124. The SDGs (==2015==) have **17** goals and **169** targets; the gender goal is **SDG 5** (MCQ 2026).
125. **SDG target 5.4** asks states to **recognise and value unpaid care** work.
126. Pakistan's National Assembly adopted the SDGs as its national agenda in **February** ==2016==.
